[{:db/id "e74084bf-bce3-5cce-92df-5af7ef7afc55" :answer/id #uuid "e74084bf-bce3-5cce-92df-5af7ef7afc55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7e7a9e73-8239-57a5-91ac-ff32d7a2cede" :answer/id #uuid "7e7a9e73-8239-57a5-91ac-ff32d7a2cede" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "631f9a35-a275-5f5b-a43f-98f0ce6e551d" :answer/id #uuid "631f9a35-a275-5f5b-a43f-98f0ce6e551d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "1aeda563-1ee7-5754-b7de-2251839a6fb2" :answer/id #uuid "1aeda563-1ee7-5754-b7de-2251839a6fb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "6c914306-27c3-556d-835c-7f1812c10b58" :answer/id #uuid "6c914306-27c3-556d-835c-7f1812c10b58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "13733091-104b-5bc3-8d88-b58877c178b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "13733091-104b-5bc3-8d88-b58877c178b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["e74084bf-bce3-5cce-92df-5af7ef7afc55" "7e7a9e73-8239-57a5-91ac-ff32d7a2cede" "631f9a35-a275-5f5b-a43f-98f0ce6e551d" "1aeda563-1ee7-5754-b7de-2251839a6fb2" "6c914306-27c3-556d-835c-7f1812c10b58"] :answer-field/correct "1aeda563-1ee7-5754-b7de-2251839a6fb2" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "25491ab2-3faf-524d-80c3-eb7b98861b8b" :question/id #uuid "25491ab2-3faf-524d-80c3-eb7b98861b8b" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y},\\,4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=8 x y,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=8 x y.\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}s &=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(16 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x^{2} y\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(24 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\left[12 y^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["13733091-104b-5bc3-8d88-b58877c178b7"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "807bda25-3871-5a18-ad95-5158c9246bea" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186172101.py"} [:db/add 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/question-generator "generator"] {:db/id 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/questions ["25491ab2-3faf-524d-80c3-eb7b98861b8b"]} [:db/ensure 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/validate]]
